<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#phalanx">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phalanx</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A phalanx is a dense formation of heavily armed infantry fighting in close ranks. Early forms are depicted on the Sumerian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/standard-of-ur">Standard of Ur</a>, an infantry tradition the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> supplemented with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarissa">sarissa</a> phalanx armed with long pikes, the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>'s army, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> inherited.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phalanx.jpg" alt="Phalanx" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ernest de Sarzec · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_of_the_vultures_(phalanx).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
